'''Opuntioideae''' is a [[Family (taxonomy)|subfamily]] of the [[cactus]] family, [[Cactus|Cactaceae]]. It contains 15 genera divided into five [[Tribe (biology)|tribes]].  The subfamily encompasses roughly 220–250 species, and is geographically distributed throughout the [[New World]] from [[Canada]], to [[Argentina]].<ref>{{Cite book|title=The Cactus Family|last=Anderson|first=Edward F.|publisher=Timber Press|year=2001|isbn=0881924989|location=Portland, OR}}</ref> Members of this subfamily have diverse [[Habit (biology)|habits]], including small [[geophytes]], hemispherical cushions, [[shrub]]s, trees, and columnar cacti consisting of indeterminate branches or determinate [[terete]] or spherical segments.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal|last1=Ritz|first1=C.M.|last2=Reiker|first2=J.|last3=Charles|first3=G.|last4=Hoxey|first4=P.|last5=Hunt|first5=D.|last6=Lowry|first6=M.|last7=Stuppy|first7=W.|last8=Taylor|first8=N.|title=Molecular phylogeny and character evolution in terete-stemmed Andean opuntias (Cactaceae−Opuntioideae)|journal=Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution|volume=65|issue=2|pages=668–681|doi=10.1016/j.ympev.2012.07.027|pmid=22877645|year=2012|bibcode=2012MolPE..65..668R }}</ref>

==Description==
Synapomorphies of Opuntioideae include small deciduous, barbed spines called [[glochid]]s born on [[areole]]s and a bony [[aril]] surrounding a campylotropous [[ovule]] (inverted and curved, such that the [[Micropyle (botany)|micropyle]] almost meets the [[Funiculus (plant reproduction)|funiculus]]).<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal|last1=Griffith|first1=M. Patrick|last2=Porter|first2=J. Mark|date=2009-01-01|title=Phylogeny of Opuntioideae (Cactaceae)|journal=International Journal of Plant Sciences|volume=170|issue=1|pages=107–116|doi=10.1086/593048|issn=1058-5893}}</ref> Other prominent morphological characters for this subfamily are presence of cylindrical, [[caducous]] leaves that tend to be shed by maturity and the sectioning of the stem into joints or pads known as [[cladode]]s.

Opuntioideae are unique among cacti for lacking in the stem a thick [[Cortex (botany)|cortex]], an extensive system of cortical bundles, collapsible cortical cells, and medullary bundles. Typically, the [[Epidermis (botany)|epidermis]] consists of a single layer of irregularly shaped cells, a cuticle at least 1-2 microns thick, and long, uniseriate [[trichome]]s in the areoles. Opuntioideae have a hypodermis of at least one layer, very thick walls, and druses (aggregations of calcium oxalate crystals), and their cortical cells have enlarged nuclei; the reason for this is unknown. They also possess [[mucilage]] cells.

Notably, their lack of collapsible cortical cells, ribs, and [[tubercle]]s mean that they cannot absorb water or transfer it intercellularly as easily as the other cacti, so this may place evolutionary constraints on the aridity of habitats and maximum adult size. One adaptation around this problem is the evolution of flattened cladodes that allow opuntioids to swell up with water, increasing in volume without an increase in surface area risking water loss. Opuntioids also lack fiber caps to their [[phloem]] bundles, which in other cacti protect against sucking insects and stiffen developing [[internodes]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Mauseth|first=James D.|date=2005-12-01|title=Anatomical features, other than wood, in subfamily opuntioideae (cactaceae)|journal=Haseltonia|volume=11|pages=113–125|doi=10.2985/1070-0048(2005)11[113:afotwi]2.0.co;2|issn=1070-0048}}</ref>

== Taxonomy ==
{{See also|Taxonomy of the Cactaceae}}

A major challenge in Opuntioideae classification is that the subfamily is known to [[Hybrid (biology)|hybridize]] (particularly the Opuntieae tribe), which complicates identifying species.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Majure|first1=Lucas|last2=Judd|first2=Walter|last3=Soltis|first3=Pam|last4=Soltis|first4=Doug|date=2012-02-14|title=Cytogeography of the Humifusa clade of Opuntia s.s. Mill. 1754 (Cactaceae, Opuntioideae, Opuntieae): correlations with pleistocene refugia and morphological traits in a polyploid complex|journal=Comparative Cytogenetics|volume=6|issue=1|pages=53–77|doi=10.3897/compcytogen.v6i1.2523|pmid=24260652|pmc=3833768|issn=1993-078X |doi-access=free }}</ref> Genera exhibit variation in [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]], which makes using genetic analysis more important in determining relationships, since physical characteristics used to define genera may be unseen in some of them.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Bárcenas|first=Rolando T.|date=August 2016|title=A molecular phylogenetic approach to the systematics of Cylindropuntieae (Opuntioideae, Cactaceae)|journal=Cladistics|language=en|volume=32|issue=4|pages=351–359|doi=10.1111/cla.12135|doi-access=free|pmid=34740297 }}</ref>

In 2009, a study by Griffith and Porter, based on [[ribosomal intergenic spacer analysis]] defined four clades within the Opuntioideae:<ref name=":1" />
# Core ''Maihueniopsis'' - which was shown as [[monophyletic]] through genetic analysis
# ''Pterocactus'' -  defined by a winged seed
# Terete-stemmed - defined by cylinder-shaped stems
# Flat-stemmed - defined by flat stems
They noted that the boundaries of some genera as then [[Circumscription (taxonomy)|circumscribed]] were unclear.<ref name=":1" />

Later research found different relationships based on what genetic sequences and analysis was used.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hernandez-Hernandez|first1=T.|last2=Hernandez|first2=H. M.|last3=De-Nova|first3=J. A.|last4=Puente|first4=R.|last5=Eguiarte|first5=L. E.|last6=Magallon|first6=S.|date=2010-12-23|title=Phylogenetic relationships and evolution of growth form in Cactaceae (Caryophyllales, Eudicotyledoneae)|journal=American Journal of Botany|volume=98|issue=1|pages=44–61|doi=10.3732/ajb.1000129|pmid=21613084|issn=0002-9122|doi-access=}}</ref> In 2010, Nyffeler and Eggli, as part of a larger overhaul of Cactaceae systematics, proposed recognizing only Cylindropuntieae and Opuntieae as true tribes of Opuntioideae, since they were the tribes generally agreed to be monophyletic. All other genera would be placed in one [[Polyphyly|polyphyletic]] [[Basal (phylogenetics)|basal]] group until further research produced more definitive answers to their phylogeny.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Nyffeler, Retro|first=Eggli, Urs|date=2010|title=A Farewell to Dated Ideas and Concepts: Molecular Phylogenetics and a Revised Suprageneric Classification of the family Cactaceae|url=https://www.zora.uzh.ch/id/eprint/43285/1/Nyffeler1.pdf|journal=Schumannia|volume=6|pages=109–149|via=uzh.ch|archive-date=2021-07-24|access-date=2019-12-21|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210724110927/https://www.zora.uzh.ch/id/eprint/43285/1/Nyffeler1.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

